Hi Mauricio,

That was likely a misnomer on my end. The 'posterior SD' portion really should be interpreted as a posterior standard error (PSE) as the sampling variability estimate for the posterior is based on the curvature at the mode of the posterior (MAP), which is the same as how traditional ML estimation theory functions. Posterior SDs really require the EAP computations, which is generally only feasible with MCMC. In any event for IRT models the EAP/MAP are generally extremely close for most models, as are the PSD and PSE, so in large-sample inferences the information will be nearly identical. Hence, mirt isn't really full-blown Bayesian when priors are included, but maybe a "poor-man's Bayesian analysis" instead as the full picture of the posterior is never drawn. HTH.

Phil

