On Sun, Apr 18, 2021 at 6:51 AM Afshin Khosravi <afshinkho...@gmail.com> wrote:
>
> Hello Everybody,
>
> I tried to run the dichotomous Rasch model with ‘mirt’ and a commercial Rasch program to compare the outputs. The item and person parameters were not close at all. The constraint on my software for model identification is that the mean of difficulty parameters is set to zero. But I think in mirt the mean of person parameters is set to zero. I guess this is the reason for the discrepancy.

One reason for sure. The other is likely that mirt uses a slope intercept form of the logistic model, logit(theta + d), rather than the usual logit(theta - b) that you would see in the literature. Just multiply the d values by -1 to get the difficulty parameters, or use coef(mod, IRTpars = TRUE) to let the package transform the coefficients and SEs.

>
> How can I set the mean of item difficulties to zero in ‘mirt’?

If you insist, see this wiki page <https://philchalmers.github.io/mirt/html/Three-Rasch.html>. But this is just an identification constraint, so it's generally not required and will only make a difference if you plan on adding Bayesian prior distributions as these are parameterization dependent.

>
> Does ‘d’ refer to item difficulty parameters?

It's an intercept parameter. HTH.

Phil

